## FABLE-007 — the score

`tools/build_music.py` writes the whole soundtrack from scratch: numpy
oscillators, Karplus-Strong plucked strings for the oud, additive flute with
breath noise for the ney, pitch-swept membrane percussion, a detuned drone, and a
Schroeder reverb. No samples, no recordings, nothing third-party — public domain
by construction, the same way `ambient_reach.ogg` was made. Rebuild any time with
`python3 tools/build_music.py` (needs numpy; ffmpeg you already have).

One motif carries all three tracks so the game sounds like one place. It is
stated in **D Hijaz** (D Eb F# G A Bb C) over harmony in **D Dorian** — the
ornate mode on top of the plain one is where "Arabian nights" meets the modal,
cadence-free feel of the old RuneScape tracks.

| track | where | length | size |
|---|---|---|---|
| `hall_of_runes` | title, menu, map | 84.7s | 1.01 MB |
| `thorn_reach` | duel, arena | 68.6s | 0.68 MB |
| `stones_and_dust` | everything else | 68.6s | 0.46 MB |

**2.1 MB for the lot.** Each is rendered longer than its loop and the overflow
folded back onto the head, so reverb tails wrap and there is no seam.

Two AudioManager bugs fixed on the way, both of which would have spoiled it:

- **Streams are forced to `Loop`.** Whether an ogg loops is a per-file `.import`
  param that Godot regenerates per machine. A fresh checkout could have played
  the title theme once and then sat in silence.
- **`CrossfadeMusic` no longer restarts a track that is already playing.** It had
  no same-id guard, so menu → map → menu reset the music to bar one every time —
  the opposite of "casually playing in the background".

Scene-to-track mapping lives in `AudioManager` itself, watching `CurrentScene`,
rather than a line of wiring in each of a dozen scenes. A screen nobody thought
about gets the quiet bed instead of silence, and a new scene needs no audio code.

