   timeout 240 xvfb-run -a "$GODOT_BIN" --path client -- --capture=title_test 2>&1 | tee /tmp/title.log
   grep -iE "\[TITLE-ATMO\]|\[AudioManager\] scene|shader|error" /tmp/title.log
   ```
   - `[TITLE-ATMO] shafts+shimmer+40 motes+vignette (reduce_motion=False, hero=yes)`
   - `[AudioManager] scene 'Main' -> music 'hall_of_runes'`
   - **No line mentioning a shader error.**
   Post `title_test.png`. A still will not show the motion, but it will show the
   light shafts, the vignette and the new button plates — if the buttons still
   look like flat brown rectangles, the styling did not take.

4. `bash tools/ux_gate.sh`.

5. On the phone, in this order:
   - **Title**: light should move across the hall, dust should drift upward,
     buttons should warm under the finger and sink when pressed. Music starts.
   - **Enter a duel**: music should *crossfade* to the duel track, not restart
     and not double up.
   - **Back to the map**: the title theme should come back — and if you then go
     menu → map → menu it must NOT restart from the beginning each time.
   - **Tutorial**: the gold frame should sit squarely on the hand card, following
     its angle on the fan.
   - **Win a duel with cards in hand**: nothing on the victory panel should
     overlap; "Turns taken" and the Rewards block should be clearly above the
     buttons.

## What I could not check

No dotnet, no Godot and no speakers here. The C# was balance-checked, symbol
cross-referenced and independently reviewed. The music I verified by rendering
spectrograms and reading them as images — which is how I found that my first pass
had plucked notes decaying in 0.15s (they were clicks, not notes, and two of the
three tracks were drum-and-drone with no tune). The numbers I can stand behind:
no clipping after decode, every loop seam smaller than the worst interior
transient, and melodic content across the full length of all three.

The three GLSL shaders are the least verified thing in this patch. They are short
and use nothing exotic, but if one fails to compile you will get a runtime error
and a missing layer rather than a build failure — which is why step 3 greps for
it specifically.
